WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.235
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:03.910
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:05.200
My name's Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.640
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.080
And I'm joined here with a
friend and colleague of mine,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.370
Ihor Dvoretskyi, who is
the SIG co-chair of SIG PM,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.842
and CNCF's representation
here at the conference, right?

00:00:19.842 --> 00:00:20.800
IHOR DVORETSKYI: Right.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.396
Yeah.

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:22.520
My name is Ihor Dvoretskyi.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.550
I'm a developer
advocate at CNCF.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.240
And I am co-chair of SIG
PM, the Product Management

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.020
SIG within the
Kubernetes community.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.360
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.651
So I want to talk about SIG PM.

00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:33.880
So SIG stands for
Special Interest Groups.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.190
PM is Product Management.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.730
You're the co-chair.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:38.510
Full disclosure.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.349
I've participated in
that community a bit.

00:00:40.349 --> 00:00:42.640
I'd like you to tell us a
little bit about the charter,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.844
and the definition
of that group.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:45.760
IHOR DVORETSKYI: Sure.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.760
So let's start with from
the entire community, how

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.910
Kubernetes community is formed,
and what are the atomic units

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.430
of Kubernetes community.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.380
So SIG is a special
interest group.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.110
It's a small unit, but a
basic unit of community.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.000
So every single
piece of Kubernetes

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.090
is being developed by some
special interest group.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:08.672
There can be some technical
and vertical SIGs.

00:01:08.672 --> 00:01:10.255
They are called the
vertical SIGs that

00:01:10.255 --> 00:01:12.460
are focused on some
specific technical error,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.720
and some specific
component of Kubernetes.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.980
For example, if you are looking
for the special interest

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.960
group which is developing,
for example, networking

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.710
functionality or
storage functionality,

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.600
there are SIG network or SIG
storage within the Kubernetes

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.100
community.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:27.310
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.080
I find that super unique, right?

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.530
I mean, it makes perfect sense
to have a special interest

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.750
group working on networking,
or working on storage,

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.200
or working on GPUs
or accelerators.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.440
But this is a special interest
workgroup for an open source

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.710
project that's a group of
product managers from lots

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.880
of competing companies working
on how to improve product

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.762
management around Kubernetes.

00:01:50.762 --> 00:01:51.720
IHOR DVORETSKYI: Right.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.970
So product management
group is really

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.620
a unique group within
the Kubernetes community,

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.020
and within the entire
open source world.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.640
Because, usually, open source
projects are simply projects.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.890
But now some companies are
building their own projects

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.280
on top of them.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.100
We have a goal of
building Kubernetes

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.650
as a solid product,
even a fully open source

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.290
that other companies that
are commercial companies

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.630
can reuse them in their
product offerings.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.130
And we have multiple
representatives

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.296
from the multiple companies
who are working together

00:02:20.296 --> 00:02:22.120
in this single direction.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.740
So SIG PM is a product
management SIG.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.210
Well, it was
established originally

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.020
as a working group,
not as a SIG.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.810
So we've been simply
the group of people

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.150
who've been interested in
working on the features

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.560
and roadmap for Kubernetes.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.510
And we've established the
Kubernetes product management

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:42.380
working group around
two years ago.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.530
And actually some
Google representatives

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:49.497
were one of the
initiators of this drive.

00:02:49.497 --> 00:02:51.580
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: What do
you see as the key output

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.014
of this group?

00:02:53.014 --> 00:02:54.430
IHOR DVORETSKYI:
So the key output

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.870
for us is driving the features
and developing a solid roadmap

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.640
for Kubernetes for
the new year's time.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.640
For the new year's here.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.380
Probably for the next few years.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:08.830
And another output is to
provide some solid information

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:12.320
for the end users, and for the
render companies who will reuse

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.571
Kubernetes for their purposes.

00:03:13.571 --> 00:03:15.570
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So just
like product management

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.350
at any one of our
independent companies,

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.680
Google, Microsoft, any of
the other contributors,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.699
but for the community,
for the CNCF community.

00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:23.740
IHOR DVORETSKYI: Exactly.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.380
So we have two
major touch points.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.605
From one side we are speaking
with the end user community,

00:03:29.605 --> 00:03:32.830
with the people who are really
using it and consuming it.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.110
And from the other side,
we are working closely

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.360
with the technical teams who are
developing some functionality.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.620
So as the SIG PM, we are simply
the proxy between the end

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:45.330
users, between vendors, between
their service providers that

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.160
are building some services and
products on top of Kubernetes,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.200
and the technical groups
who are developing

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.720
some functionality
for Kubernetes

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:52.637
and within Kubernetes.

00:03:52.637 --> 00:03:53.553
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:03:53.553 --> 00:03:54.890
This is a fascinating project.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.680
And I hope that if this
group is successful,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.640
we'll see similar groups
emerge around other open source

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:01.240
projects.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.356
Ihor, thank you very
much for your time.

00:04:03.356 --> 00:04:04.480
IHOR DVORETSKYI: Thank you.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

